.Dd August 21, 2026
.Dt VV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vv
.Nd print a tool version as a strict SemVer triple
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar tool
.Pp
.Nm
.Fl -verified-version
.Pp
.Nm
.Fl -quarantine
.Op Ar tool
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints exactly one
.Li X.Y.Z
line for
.Ar tool .
Missing or unparseable versions produce
.Li 0.0.0 .
Normal lookups always exit zero.
.Pp
Lookup first reads a declared version file at
.Pa <prefix>/share/vv/<tool>
without executing the tool, then checks the quarantine registry
.Pq Sx QUARANTINE
for a small number of tools that need different handling, then on macOS reads
an application bundle's
.Pa Info.plist
without executing the tool, then tries the strict
.Fl -verified-version
protocol, then a tool-agnostic ladder of common version flags
.Pq Fl -version , Fl version , Cm version , Fl V , Fl v .
Declared files and
.Pa Info.plist
fields are trusted sources and may contain a bare integer. The strict protocol
and generic ladder require a dotted numeric version. Any other result is
.Li 0.0.0 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -verified-version , Fl -version
Print the
.Nm
version.
.It Fl -quarantine
List every quarantined tool, its action, and its reason.
.It Fl -quarantine Ar tool
Explain one quarantined tool: its reason and how to leave the list. Exits zero
whether or not
.Ar tool
is quarantined.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print brief help.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width VV_TIMEOUT
.It Ev VV_TIMEOUT
Probe timeout in seconds. Default: 5.
.El
.Sh QUARANTINE
.Nm
keeps a small, inline, documented registry of tools that misbehave under
generic probing or misreport their version through
.Pa Info.plist .
Each entry names one tool, one action, and one reason. There are two actions:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm flag=Ar x
Probe only the single flag
.Ar x ,
never the generic ladder. Used when a tool's usual
.Fl -version
flag has a side effect, such as
.Xr pico 1
opening an editor buffer instead of printing a version.
.It Cm exec
Run the strict protocol and generic ladder even though the tool resolves
inside a macOS application bundle. Used when a bundle's
.Pa Info.plist
reports the bundling application's version rather than the tool's own, such as
the Docker-family CLIs bundled inside Rancher Desktop.
.El
.Pp
A quarantine entry is matched first by the invoked name, then by the resolved
real binary's name, so an alias inherits its target's entry (for example macOS
.Xr nano 1 ,
a symlink to
.Xr pico 1 )
while a distinct binary that merely shares a name (GNU
.Xr nano 1 )
is left to the normal ladder.
.Pp
A declared
.Pa share/vv/<tool>
file always wins over a quarantine entry. Run
.Nm Fl -quarantine
to list every quarantined tool, or
.Nm Fl -quarantine Ar tool
to see one entry's reason, action, and applicable fix. For a
.Cm flag=
entry, the preferred upstream fix is
.Fl -verified-version ;
a safe conventional
.Fl -version
is also accepted after the entry is removed. For an
.Cm exec
entry, the tool must publish a declaration, provide authoritative bundle
metadata, or install its CLI outside the application bundle before the
exception can be removed.
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
.Nm
follows one generic lookup for every tool, plus the small documented
quarantine list above for tools that are unsafe to probe generically or that
misreport their version. A tool may always become compatible by shipping a
.Pa share/vv/<tool>
version file. Outside an application bundle, it may instead implement
.Fl -verified-version
or expose a safe conventional version command for the generic flag ladder.
Only a dotted numeric version is accepted from the ladder, so a bare integer
in help text is never mistaken for a version. A tool that reports no parseable
version resolves to
.Li 0.0.0 .
There are no user recipe or parser configuration files.
.Pp
A tool that resolves to a path inside a macOS application bundle
.Pq Pa *.app
is never executed unless it is quarantined with the
.Cm exec
action. Such a tool resolves through its bundle's
.Pa Info.plist
.Pq Sy CFBundleShortVersionString , falling back to Sy CFBundleVersion ,
or through a
.Pa share/vv/<tool>
file, which is also read without execution.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The utility exits zero for every normal result, including missing or
unparseable tools, and for
.Fl -quarantine
queries. Invalid options exit two. Any other non-zero status indicates a
defect in
.Nm .
